Yes.

22:11.360 --> 22:14.860
The framework transitions into a computational model known as fear pop.

22:15.400 --> 22:19.580
And it completely redefines how we think about software bugs and system crashes.

22:19.940 --> 22:22.560
Normally, we think of software checking for errors locally.

22:22.560 --> 22:27.020
Like, I type my password in and the code runs a quick differential check.

22:27.560 --> 22:30.360
Does this string of text match the stored string?

22:30.920 --> 22:33.300
Or is this variable an integer?

22:33.800 --> 22:35.880
It's just looking at the immediate next step.

22:36.020 --> 22:38.400
Traditional computing is almost entirely differential.

22:38.840 --> 22:42.240
It asks, is the move I'm about to make legal right now?

22:42.840 --> 22:46.200
But sphere pop introduces this concept of the admissibility log,

22:46.500 --> 22:48.800
or what we'd normally just call an event log.

22:49.060 --> 22:52.140
And my understanding of an event log was always that it's a receipt.

22:52.140 --> 22:53.320
A static list?

22:53.520 --> 22:56.560
Yeah, it's a passive text file sitting on a server somewhere that just says,

22:57.000 --> 23:00.220
at 2.04 p.m., the user clicked add to cart.

23:00.560 --> 23:02.520
Just a historical record of what happened.

23:02.640 --> 23:05.980
And this is where sphere pop introduces a radical paradigm shift.

23:06.500 --> 23:08.640
In this model, the event log is not passive.

23:08.920 --> 23:09.700
It is not a receipt.

23:10.020 --> 23:13.420
It is an active filtration constraint on the state space of the program.

23:13.580 --> 23:15.020
I love the maze analogy for this.

23:15.200 --> 23:20.440
Instead of a receipt, think of the event log as the literal walls of a maze you are running through.

23:20.620 --> 23:21.120
That's good.

